---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { ROUTES } from '../lib/routes.mjs';
import { buildTitle, buildDescription, BRAND, AUTHOR } from '../lib/seo.mjs';

const title = buildTitle('Editorial Policy: What Marsad Cyber Will Not Publish');
const description = buildDescription(
  'Marsad Cyber never names an affected organisation. The legal basis, the three publication levels, and the automated checks that enforce them on every build.',
);
const crumbs = [{ label: 'Home', href: ROUTES.home() }, { label: 'Editorial Policy', href: ROUTES.editorialPolicy() }];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} crumbs={crumbs} current={{ kind: 'page', slug: 'editorial-policy' }}>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>Editorial Policy</h1>
    <div class="byline">
      <span class="byline__tag">Policy</span>
      <span class="byline__sep">/</span>
      <span>Applies to every page on this site</span>
      <span class="byline__by"><span class="byline__monogram" aria-hidden="true">MC</span>By <a href={ROUTES.about()}>{AUTHOR}</a></span>
    </div>


    <p class="lede">
      {BRAND} publishes aggregate ransomware data about the United Arab Emirates and never
      identifies an affected organisation. This page states that rule, the reasoning behind it,
      and the mechanism that enforces it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">

    <h2>The rule</h2>
    <div class="callout">
      <p class="callout__title">Aggregate is the default and only mode.</p>
      <p>
        Published: sector, ransomware group, date and counts. Never published: the name of an
        affected organisation, its domain, the contents or volume of leaked data, screenshots of
        leak sites, <code>.onion</code> addresses, or the names of employees.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Why</h2>
    <p>
      UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2021 on Combatting Rumours and Cybercrimes treats the
      disclosure, leaking, publication and republication of unlawfully obtained electronic
      personal data as an offence under Article 6. Articles 52 and 54 address the dissemination of
      false information capable of harming the economy or the public interest. Data published on
      ransomware leak sites is, by definition, unverified: it is an attacker's assertion, not an
      established fact.
    </p>
    <p>
      The law is not the only reason. This site is read by the security leadership of
      organisations in the United Arab Emirates. A resource that publicly names local victims
      reads as a threat to that reader rather than as expertise, and it would compromise the
      independence this project depends on.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is also no analytical cost. Aggregate figures answer the questions people actually
      ask, such as which sectors are targeted and which groups are active. A page about a single
      named organisation answers none of them.
    </p>

    <h2>The one exception</h2>
    <p>
      An organisation may be named only where the incident has already been reported by a
      licensed UAE news outlet, and only with attribution to that outlet as the source. In that
      case the report is the source, not the leak site. No such naming appears on this site at
      present.
    </p>

    <h2>How the rule is enforced</h2>
    <p>
      The rule is not a matter of editorial care. Identifying fields are removed when data is
      collected, before anything is stored, so they do not exist in the files that build these
      pages. A separate automated check scans every built page against fingerprints of the removed
      values and fails the build on any match, as it does on any <code>.onion</code> address.
      A build that would identify an organisation cannot be published.
    </p>

    <h2>Independence and scope</h2>
    <p>
      {BRAND} is an independent threat-data research project. It is not affiliated with, endorsed
      by, or acting on behalf of any vendor, regulator, or government body of the United Arab
      Emirates, and it holds no accreditation from any of them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where this site describes regulatory frameworks such as the Dubai Information Security
      Regulation or the UAE Information Assurance Standard, it describes what those documents
      require. It does not offer certification against them and does not claim to assess
      compliance with them.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Every figure traces to a dated snapshot held in version control, and the calculation behind
      it is reproducible. Where a figure is wrong, it is corrected and the snapshot history shows
      what changed. See the <a href={ROUTES.methodology()}>methodology</a> for how figures are
      derived and what their known limitations are.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
